The isolated export was built from .tools/glow-release-probe-final-20261013/project using the pinned Godot 4.7.2 Windows release template. Its embedded source manifest hashes copied source and input files after overlay/configuration edits and before export. Those maps exactly match the debug working-tree maps; the external build manifest's embedded-manifest digest matches the runtime-reported digest. The app project.godot and export_presets.cfg hashes are recorded separately.

A persistent climb-trim watcher was observed immediately after release and before debug (PID 88100, --path app --script research/flight-dynamics/climb-trim/watch.gd). It was not stopped. No independent CPU isolation or per-run load sampling was performed, so host isolation remains unqualified.

Interpretation: release timings are materially lower than debug timings, but the 500 us p95 target remains unmet for both glow families. Legacy release is near but above the target. Results are diagnostic, not a release-gate certification or physical claim. The candidate propeller polar is a uniform-box surrogate derived from inventory; crank polars are inferred at 2e-5 kg m2 (2T) and 3e-5 kg m2 (4T), without matched measurements.
